---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timeline from '../../components/Timeline.astro';
import { MIN_COMMITS, experiments } from '../../lib/timeline';
import { RUNGS } from '../../content.config';

const chapters = (await getCollection('journey')).sort((a, b) => a.id.localeCompare(b.id));

// The context ledger, one row per chapter that has earned one. Two bands carry
// no ledger and are excluded rather than rendered as rows of blanks: the
// repo-less 90- chapters, which have no repo to read one off, and the seven stub
// chapters, which gave theirs up in inc5g because a ledger claim with an empty
// `## Artifact` is the table asserting evidence the chapter does not show. Every
// key is `.optional()` for exactly this reason. A stub earns its row back by
// writing the artifact; `tests/content.test.ts` holds that order.
// The rung table renders BY MAPPING over `RUNGS`, never from six hand-typed
// rows: order and vocabulary come from the enum, so neither can drift. The
// Record is declared total over `RUNGS` for the editor's benefit, but note what
// actually stops a bad ship: `astro build` does NOT typecheck, so a seventh
// rung added to `src/content.config.ts` without a date and a unit fails the
// build at RENDER time (`Cannot read properties of undefined`), demoed red
// 2026-09-09. That message names neither the rung nor this file, which is why
// `tests/content.test.ts` also asserts the pairing — the test is the legible
// half of the gate, the build is the one that cannot be skipped.
const RUNG_META: Record<(typeof RUNGS)[number], { from: string; unit: string }> = {
  asking: { from: '2025-06', unit: 'a question' },
  suggesting: { from: '2025-06', unit: 'a snippet' },
  delegating: { from: '2025-07', unit: 'a task' },
  planning: { from: '2026-02', unit: 'a plan' },
  configuring: { from: '2026-04', unit: 'a policy' },
  governing: { from: '2026-07', unit: 'a process' },
};

const LEDGER_FIELDS = [
  { key: 'could_see', label: 'could see', concern: 'Control' },
  { key: 'retrieved', label: 'retrieved', concern: 'Control' },
  { key: 'versioned', label: 'versioned', concern: 'Provenance' },
  { key: 'verified_by', label: 'verified by', concern: 'Quality' },
  { key: 'cost_to_look', label: 'cost to look', concern: 'Cost' },
] as const;

const LEDGER_KEYS = LEDGER_FIELDS.map((field) => field.key);
const repoBacked = chapters.filter((entry) => entry.data.repo !== undefined);
const ledgerRows = repoBacked.filter((entry) =>
  LEDGER_KEYS.some((key) => entry.data[key] !== undefined),
);
---
<Base
  title="The journey · ai-coding-journey"
  description="One chapter per repo, from a 20-year coding pause to a ~5k-commit app built with AI agents."
>
  <h1>The journey</h1>
  <p class="lede">One chapter per repo, oldest first.</p>

  <Timeline variant="full" />

  <ol class="chapters">
    {chapters.map((entry) => (
      <li>
        <a href={`/journey/${entry.id}/`}>{entry.data.title}</a>
        {/* A non-repo chapter (90- band) carries none of the git-derived keys. */}
        {entry.data.stage && <span class="stage">{entry.data.stage}</span>}
        {entry.data.commits !== undefined && (
          <span class="commits">{entry.data.commits.toLocaleString('en-US')} commits</span>
        )}
        {entry.data.start && entry.data.end && (
          <span class="span">{entry.data.start} → {entry.data.end}</span>
        )}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <h2>The six rungs</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The rungs are named off the <em>unit of instruction</em> — what is handed to the machine in one
    go. It is the one dimension that moves at every step, which is why it names the ladder rather
    than the tool, the model or the interface, all of which change out of order.
  </p>
  <div class="ledger-scroll">
    <table class="ledger">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">#</th>
          <th scope="col">rung</th>
          <th scope="col">from</th>
          <th scope="col">unit handed over</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {RUNGS.map((rung, index) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{index + 1}</td>
            <th scope="row">{rung}</th>
            <td>{RUNG_META[rung].from}</td>
            <td>{RUNG_META[rung].unit}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="lede">
    The three upper boundaries are git-dated, not authored. Rung 4 opens 2026-02-28, the first
    token this account spent in Claude Code. Rung 5 opens 2026-04-05, the first commit of the
    dotfiles repo, which is the day configuration itself entered version control. Rung 6 opens
    2026-07-09, the day the skills directory did the same. Each is a date something was recorded,
    so the seams can be checked rather than argued with.
  </p>

  <h2>What the machine could see</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Five fields per chapter, each drawn from a closed set, each read off the repo as it stood
    the fortnight it opened — not as it ended. The rung a repo reached later is its own key.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    {ledgerRows.length} of the {repoBacked.length} repo-backed chapters appear here. A chapter
    enters the table by showing the artifact its ledger claims — quoted or fenced, not described.
    The other {repoBacked.length - ledgerRows.length} have a repo and a rung but no evidence
    written up yet, and a row of five unbacked claims would be the table asserting exactly what it
    exists to prove.
  </p>
  <div class="ledger-scroll">
    <table class="ledger">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">chapter</th>
          {LEDGER_FIELDS.map((field) => (
            <th scope="col">
              {field.label}
              <span class="concern">{field.concern}</span>
            </th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ledgerRows.map((entry) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={`/journey/${entry.id}/`}>{entry.data.title}</a></th>
            {LEDGER_FIELDS.map((field) => (
              <td>{entry.data[field.key] ?? <span class="unset">—</span>}</td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Also tried</h2>
  <p class="lede">Repos with fewer than {MIN_COMMITS} commits — no chapter of their own.</p>
  <ul class="experiments">
    {experiments.map((row) => (
      <li>
        <span class="repo">{row.repo}</span>
        <span class="span">{row.first_commit}</span>
        <span class="commits">{row.commits} commits</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { color: var(--fg-dim); margin-top: 0; }
  .chapters, .experiments { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .chapters li, .experiments li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(10rem, 1fr) 9rem 7rem auto;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: var(--space-2) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .stage, .commits, .span, .repo {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-0);
    color: var(--fg-dim);
  }
  .repo { color: var(--fg); }
  /* Six columns of hyphenated enum values do not fit a phone. Scroll the table
     rather than abbreviating it: the values are the vocabulary the rest of the
     site uses, and a legend would cost the reader a lookup on every cell. */
  .ledger-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .ledger {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-0);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ledger th, .ledger td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-weight: normal;
  }
  .ledger thead th { color: var(--fg); vertical-align: bottom; }
  .ledger tbody th { color: var(--fg); }
  .ledger td { color: var(--fg-dim); }
  .ledger .concern {
    display: block;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }
  .ledger .unset { color: var(--fg-dim); }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .chapters li, .experiments li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-1); }
  }
</style>
